===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wreyt-}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|wratthe|t=anger, hostility, distress retribution}}{{nb...|wraaþ, wragh, wraght, wraith, wrat, wrā̆th, wrathe, wrā̆the, wrath, wraththe, wratth, wratthe, wraugth, wretthe, (Scotland) wraith, (Southwest Midlands) wradðe, wrað, wraðe, wræððe, wraððe, wraððhe, wraðþe, wraþe, wraþþe, wreadðe, wreaððe, wredða, wredðe, wreþþe|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=wratthe|pos=n|id=MED53562}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|wrǣþþu|t=ire, wrath}}{{nb...|wraððo, wræððu, wræððo, wrǣþþ, (Northumberland) urāððo|otherforms=1}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wraiþiþu|t=anger, fury, wrath}}, from {{m|gmw-pro|*wraiþ|t=angry, furious, wroth; hostile, violent; bent, twisted}} (from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wraiþaz|t=angry, furious, wroth; hostile, violent; bent, twisted}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*wreyt-|t=to twist}}) + {{m|gmw-pro|*-iþu|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|abstract noun}}s}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1086969557|date=December 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> Effectively analysable as {{suffix|en|wroth|th|id2=abstract nominal|pos2=abstract nominal suffix}}; compare {{cog|nl|wreedte}}.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|wratthen|t=to be or become angry, to rage; to quarrel; to cause wrath, offend; to become troubled or vexed; to cause grief or harm, grieve, vex}}{{nb...|wrath, wrathe, wrathen, wraththe, wraththen, wraththi, wratth, wratthe, wraþþen, wreththe, wreththi, wretth, wretthe, wretthi, wretthin, wreþþen, (Southwest England, Southwest Midlands) wreaððin, wreððen, (Early Middle English) wraðhin, wræðþiæn|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=wratthen|pos=v|id=MED53563}}</ref> from {{m|enm|wratthe}}, {{m|enm|wretthe|pos=n}} (see above)<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=9655925131|date=September 2023}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-en|pos=suffix forming the {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{glossary|verb}}s}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-en|pos=''suf.''(3)|noformat=1|id=MED13518}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|formal}} [[great#Adjective|Great]] [[anger#Noun|anger]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this.
#: {{synonyms|en|fury|ire|Thesaurus:anger}}
#: {{ux|en|{{w|Homer}} relates an episode in the Trojan War that reveals the tragic consequences of the '''wrath''' of {{w|Achilles}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Ephesians|4|31|folio=cclvij|folioend=cclvij|versoend=1|passage=Let all bitternes, fearſnes and '''wrath''', rorynge and curſyd ſpeakynge, be put awaye from you, with all maliciouſnes.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|chapter=January|folio=1|verso=1|passage=Thou barrein ground, whome winters '''wrath''' hath waſted, <br> Art made a myrrhour, to behold my plight: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|act=II|scene=ii|sig=B2|verso=1|page=30|passage=I tel you true my hart is ſwolne with '''wrath''', <br> On this ſame theeuiſh villain Tamburlain.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=I|canto=II|stanza=8|page=21|passage=[H]im ſo far had borne his light-foot ſteede, <br> Pricked vvith '''vvrath''' and fiery fierce diſdaine, <br> That him to follovv vvas but fruitleſſe paine; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|act=II|scene=i|page=183|column=1|passage=VVho (in my '''vvrath''') <br> Kneel'd and{{sic|at}} my feet, and bid me be aduis'd?}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=III|scene=iii|page=13|column=2|passage=Lingring perdition (vvorſe then any death <br> Can be at once) ſhall ſtep, by ſtep attend <br> You, and your vvayes, vvhoſe '''vvraths''', to guard you from, <br> VVhich here, in this moſt deſolate Iſle, elſe fals <br> Vpon your heads, is nothing but hearts-ſorrovv, <br> And a cleere life enſuing.}}
#* {{RQ:Joseph Beaumont Psyche|canto=II|stanza=16|page=14|column=2|passage=[S]tern briſtles hedg'd up high <br> His back, vvhich did all '''vvrath''' of thorns defie.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Wild Gallant|page=24|passage=[C]ome not near me to night, vvhile I'm in '''vvrath'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=4|lines=651–652|page=142|passage=The Seer, vvho could not yet his '''VVrath''' aſſvvage, <br> Rovvl'd his green Eyes, that ſparkl'd vvith his Rage; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|chapter=Palamon and Arcite|book=I|page=17|passage=For vvhen he knevv his Rival freed and gone, <br> He ſvvells vvith '''VVrath'''; he makes outrageous Moan: <br> He frets, he fumes, he ſtares, he ſtamps the Ground; <br> The hollowvv Tovv'r vvith Clamours rings around: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Arbuthnot|lines=29–30|page=2|passage=VVhat ''Drop'' or ''Noſtrum'' can this Plague remove? <br> Or vvhich muſt end me, a Fool's '''VVrath''' or Love?}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|poem=Expostulation|page=124|passage=Then nature injur'd, ſcandaliz'd, defil'd, <br> Unveil'd her bluſhing cheek, look'd on and ſmil'd, <br> Beheld vvith joy the lovely ſcene defac'd, <br> And prais'd the '''vvrath''' that lay'd her beauties vvaſte.}}
#* {{RQ:Burney Camilla|volume=I|chapter=A Raffle|page=254|passage={{...}} Miſs Margland, in deep '''vvrath''', refuſed to let her move a ſtep.}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Peter Bell|page=26|passage=But quickly Peter's mood is chang'd, <br> And on he drives with cheeks that burn <br> In downright fury and in '''wrath'''— {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Lady of the Lake|stanza=XXI|page=172|passage=When lo! a wasted Female form, <br> Blighted by '''wrath''' of sun and storm, <br> In tattered weeds and wild array, <br> Stood on a cliff beside the way, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Dombey and Son|chapter=Another Mother and Daughter|page=348|passage=[S]he remained still: except that she clenched her arms tighter and tighter within each other, on her bosom, as if to restrain them by that means from doing an injury to herself, or some one else, in the blind fury of the '''wrath''' that suddenly possessed her.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Philip|volume=II|chapter=I Charge You, Drop Your Daggers!|page=238|passage=His chafing, bleeding temper is one raw; his whole soul one rage, and '''wrath''', and fever.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Enoch Arden|poem=Aylmer's Field|page=87|passage=How sweetly would she glide between your '''wraths''', <br> And steal you from each other!}}
#* {{RQ:Ouida In Maremma|volume=I|chapter=XII|page=243|passage=We come in charity and good will, but we may go in '''wrath'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=Sing Cuccu Nu|page=47|passage=The most rapid and most seductive transition in all human nature is that which attends the palliation of a ravenous appetite. {{...}} Can those harmless but refined fellow-diners be the selfish cads whose gluttony and personal appearance so raised your contemptuous '''wrath''' on your arrival?}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[punishment|Punishment]], [[retribution]], or [[vengeance]] [[result#Verb|resulting]] from anger; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this.
#: {{ux|en|the '''wrath''' of God}}
#* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|Hosea|13|11|folio=lxxxvi|verso=1|column=1|passage={{...}} I [''i.e.'', God] gaue the [thee] a kinge in my '''wrath''', and in my diſpleaſure will I take him from the agayne.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Romans|13|4|page=1396|column=2|passage=[H]e is the miniſter of God, a reuenger ''to execute'' '''wrath''' vpon him that doeth euill.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Comus|page=27|passage=And though not mortall, yet a cold ſhuddring devv <br> Dips me all o'e[r], as vvhen the '''vvrath''' of ''[[w:Jupiter (god)|Iove]]'' <br> Speaks thunder, and the chaines of ''{{w|Erebus}}'' <br> To ſome of ''[[w:Saturn (mythology)|Saturns]]'' crevv.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=III|lines=406–410|page=82|passage=He to appeaſe thy '''wrauth''', and end the ſtrife <br> Of Mercy and Juſtice in thy face diſcern'd, <br> Regardleſs of the Bliſs wherein hee ſat <br> Second to thee, offerd himſelf to die <br> For mans offence.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=188|passage=[T]he '''vvrath''' of God cometh upon the children of diſobedience.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|obsolete}} Great [[ardor|ardour]] or [[passion#Noun|passion]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|act=V|scene=ii|page=194|column=2|passage=[T]hey are in the verie '''vvrath''' of loue, and they vvill together. Clubbes cannot part them.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=III|scene=iv|page=269|column=1|passage=[I]f you hold your life at any price, betake you to your gard: for your oppoſite hath in him vvhat youth, ſtrength, skill, and '''vvrath''', can furniſh man vvithall.}}

