====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|chiefly|EME|obsolete}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[anger#Verb|anger]] (someone); to [[enrage]].
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Jacobus van Gruitroede|translator=&#91;[[w:Lady Margaret Beaufort|Margaret Beaufort]]&#93;|title=The Mirroure of Golde for the Synfull Soule|url=https://www.proquest.com/eebo/docview/2240904806/99854335|location=London|publisher={{...|Emprynted at London in Fletestrete at the signe of Saynt George by}} [[w:Richard Pynson|Richarde Pynson]]|year=['''1506'''?]|section=folio 12, verso|oclc=1356160218|passage=[R]emembre howe by thy cursed synnes thou haste offended and '''wrathed''' thy lorde god.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|translator=&#91;{{w|Andrew Chertsey}}&#93;|title=[[w:Jesus|Ihesus]]. The Floure of the Commaundements of God{{nb...|with Many Examples and Auctorytees Extracte and Drawen as well of Holy Scryptures as of Other Doctours and Good Auncient Faders, the whiche is Moche Utyle and Prouffytable vnto All People. [...]}}|url=https://www.proquest.com/eebo/docview/2240925286/99852934|location=London|publisher={{...|Emprynted at London in Flete strete at the sygne of the Sonne by}} {{w|Wynkyn de Worde}}|year='''1510''' September 24 (Gregorian calendar)|section=folio 60, recto|oclc=606537040|passage=Of ire yͤ whiche is agayne god. {{...}} A man '''wratheth''' hym ayenst god for many thynges, pryncypally for the flagellacions, aduersytees, fortunes, sykenesses, & mortalytees, losses, punycyons, famyne, warre & yll tyme.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Saynt Peter of Lucẽbourth [''i.e.'', {{w|Pierre de Luxembourg}}]|translator=anonymous|title=The Boke Entytuled the Next Way to Heuen{{nb...|the whiche in True Walkỹge or Goỹnge is but Thre Dayes Iourney, and to Go or Walke Euery Daye but Thre Myles as Wytnesseth Moyses who Sayeth. Ibimus viam trium dierum in solitudinem. &c. Exodi. iij. Ca. F.}}|url=https://www.proquest.com/eebo/docview/2240894844/99849816|location=London|publisher={{...|Emprynted at London in Fletestrete at the sygne of the Sonne by}} {{w|Wynkyn de Worde}}|year=1520|section=folio &#91;4&#93;, recto|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_the-boke-entytuled-the-n_peter-of-luxenb-st_1510/page/n6/mode/1up|oclc=84761697|passage=And than the byſſhop ſayd vnto the clerke&#11805; thou haſt '''wrathed''' me&#11805; but yf thou wylte be ſory thou ſhalte haue my loue as thou haddeſt before&#11805; & I ſhall gyue the [''i.e.'', thee] the benefyce yͭ I haue promyſed to gyue the&#11805; ſholde not he be anone ſory of that I byleue that yes.}}
## To [[become]] [[angry#Adjective|angry]] with (someone).
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become angry.
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|volume=II|chapter=X|page=267|passage="Nay, '''wrath''' thee not, Will," said Ganlesse; "and speak no words in haste, lest you may have cause to repent at leisure.{{nb...}}"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Edward Bouverie Pusey|E[dward] B[ouverie] Pusey]]|chapter=[[w:Book of Nahum|Nahum]]|title=The Minor Prophets with a Commentary Explanatory and Practical and Introductions to the Several Books|series=The Holy Bible with a Commentary{{nb...|Explanatory and Practical and Introduction to the Several Books}}|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=J. H. & J. Parker,{{nb...|Oxford, & 337, Strand; Deighton, Bell & Co., Cambridge; Rivington, Waterloo Place, London.}}|year=1860|page=374|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sLQpfY9tZKgC&pg=PA374|column=2|oclc=6361977|passage=God is a righteous judge, strong and patient, and God '''wratheth''' every day.}}

